Repository evidence:
[Section: 3. Animation loop > Limitations]

- no `requestAnimationFrame`;

- no delta-time integration;

- heavy DOM layout reads/writes;

- no frame-time measurement.

[Section: 17. Source tree / runtime architecture > Architecture weaknesses]

- simulation logic, rendering and state are tightly coupled to DOM nodes;

- all-pairs collision loop scales poorly;

- timer-based loop ignores actual frame timing;

- layout reads/writes can trigger browser reflow costs;

- no separation between physics model and presentation.

[Section: 23. Mistakes / anti-patterns / likely lessons — expanded > Layout thrashing risk]

Repeatedly reading `offsetLeft/offsetTop` and writing styles can force browser layout work.

[Section: 30. Product / system failure potential]

For a visual demo, direct harm is low. Failure modes include:

- browser slowdown/high CPU;

- balls becoming visually unstable/overlapping;

- incorrect cursor interaction;

- partially offscreen initialization;

- timer throttling changing simulation behavior;

- deployment workflow never actually executing.

If the same collision code were reused for physically meaningful simulation, the lack of rigorous validation would become a much larger problem.

